# Probes promoted into the strict-corpus gate for the key 25-linux.
#
# Read by scripts/jdk-only-strict-probes.sh IN ADDITION to the three default
# probes (JdkOnlyCensusLoadProbe, JdkOnlyBreadthProbe, JdkOnlyPlatformProbe).
# The list is per-key on purpose: a probe may be promoted on one platform and
# not another, and this file is the Linux half of that. Setting PROBE_LIST in
# the environment bypasses this file entirely.
#
# THE BAR, and it is the same one the 25-windows file states: all three arms
# (HotSpot control, --real-jdk, --jdk-only) exit 0 AND the two CratonVM
# transcripts are byte-identical to the HotSpot one, over at least two runs.
# A probe that merely fails to crash is not promoted -- a divergence that is
# already present would be frozen into the gate as acceptable by the act of
# promoting it.
#
# EVIDENCE (2026-09-09, this file):
#
#   host      azure vm1, Linux 6.17.0-1022-azure x86_64, 8 cores
#   jdk       Temurin 25.0.4+7 (/data/jdkimages/jdk25-linux)
#   binary    release, built from origin/dev @ 5487287dc
#   method    one harness run PER PROBE, not one run of 64 -- a single arm
#             failure inside a shared run poisons the whole transcript, and
#             per-probe attribution is the point of a promotion list
#
#   run 1     64 of 64 CLEAN, 25435 rows, 165 s total (2.6 s mean)
#   run 2     64 of 64 CLEAN, 25435 rows
#   delta     no probe changed verdict or row count between the two runs
#
# 25435 rows is the SAME total the 25-windows promotion measured. Two
# platforms, two JDK builds, one row count: that is a stronger statement about
# the corpus than either run alone, and it is why the two files carry the same
# 64 names.
#
# COST. 165 s for all 64 on a release binary. The 25-windows file records
# +731 s and defers nothing on cost; that number was measured on a DEBUG
# binary and on Windows, so it is NOT comparable to this one and neither
# number should be quoted for the other platform.
#
# VERIFIED ON THE PROFILE CI ACTUALLY USES. Everything above was measured with
# a RELEASE binary, and ci.yml runs the strict-corpus step against
# target/debug/cratonvm -- a gate run under a different codegen profile is not
# that gate. Re-run end to end with a DEBUG binary:
#
#   25-linux debug, 64 promoted   rc=0 PASS   609 s   (host load average 27)
#   21-linux debug, 3 default     rc=0 PASS   144 s   (host load average 22)
#
# 609 s is the number to budget against, and it is pessimistic twice over: a
# debug binary, on an 8-core host that was four times oversubscribed by other
# work at the time. The same leg on a release binary at low load took 171 s and
# 195 s. If this step ever needs a timeout raised, raise it -- do not thin this
# list to fit, because the promotion is what makes the leg measure anything
# beyond the three default probes.
#
# The two probes the 25-windows file excludes on cost (FjpDeepSum, 564 s for
# 1 row; InvokeAllCount, 436 s for 7 rows) are excluded here too, and for
# consistency rather than from a Linux measurement: they were not swept here.
# Someone adding them should measure them on this host first.
AdDispatch
AdFailFast
AdRetireProbe
ArrayListShadowSweep
AsyncChannelSweep
BigIntegerSweep
BufInsertProbe
ChmElemDbg
ChmKeySetGrowth
